{
  "format": "optibench-workstation",
  "version": 1,
  "meta": {
    "appVersion": "0.1.0-alpha",
    "author": "Optibench examples",
    "created": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "description": "A circuit steers a laser beam. CIRCUIT: the function generator (0.5 Hz triangle, 0 to 5 V) is the control voltage of servo SV1 (DC-control mode, 180 degrees for 5 V, powered from the 6 V battery). An ACTUATOR link with target \"rotation\" turns the scan mirror on the optical bench: mirror angle = 30 deg + servo angle / 6, i.e. 30 to 60 degrees, so the reflected green beam sweeps +/-30 degrees across the wall screen once per second in each direction. OPTICS: in the middle of the sweep (mirror at 45 degrees) the beam crosses a small target photodiode. A DETECTOR link feeds its power to PD1; the photocurrent raises the voltage on R2 above the 1 V reference (R3/R4), the LM393 comparator U1 pulls its open-collector output low, the HIT LED flashes and the decade counter HITS counts each pass when the beam leaves the target. Try the sine waveform, a faster sweep (the servo speed limit of 600 deg/s then rounds the scan) or move the target.",
    "dimension": "2d",
    "modified": "2026-11-19T00:00:00.000Z",
    "title": "Laser Show: Servo Scan Mirror"
  },
